An unmarked entry whose `match` is a proper substring of another's, so which
of the two serves depends on where the lines sit.

This is `duplicate-match.toml` one step sideways, and the step is the whole
finding: two entries matching the SAME substring were already refused, and two
entries where one match CONTAINS the other were not, though the consequence is
identical. Every id `qwen3.6` is about, `qwen3` matches too. First-match-in-
file-order picks the winner, and an operating point is a transcribed
measurement, not something a line number should decide.

THIS IS THE DOGMA'S OWN RETIRED SPELLING, byte for byte, with nothing removed
but the `fallback = true` line the respell added. That is the point of it. The
rule was being broken by the one file it exists to protect, for as long as the
file has existed, and what stood in for the rule was a comment above the tables
explaining that `qwen3` had to be written below `qwen3.6` and `qwen3.5`. A
consumer that read the tables into an unordered map -- which that comment
warned against, in prose, having no way to refuse it -- got `qwen3` for a 3.6
model, whose `thinking_kwarg = false` is the control that does not work on that
family.

Refused since 2026-09-13, with the dogma's respell, and the two had to land in
one commit: enabling the refusal first would have refused the dogma, and
respelling first would have left the rule optional.

The detector folds case, because `serves` folds and `duplicate-match-by-case`
is the fixture for what happens when a detector does not: `Qwen3` shadowing
`qwen3.6` is the same defect wearing the same disguise that one already caught.
